      "score": 83,
      "summary": "Output A is honest about what static evidence can show and gives a clear priority order. It catches the hover writes, the lost grab offset, the missing pointer capture, the competing transitions and the scaling. It is thinner on velocity: it doesn't mention units, handing release velocity into the settle, or projected endpoints. Its interruption guidance is brief. Its Reduced Motion path is sound but less detailed than the others."
    },
    "design-craft": {
      "blind_label": "B",
      "criteria": {
        "accessibility": 10,
        "actionability": 9,
        "direct_manipulation": 15,
        "evidence_honesty": 15,
        "interruption": 15,
        "motion_necessity": 8,
        "performance": 10,
        "velocity_projection": 15
      },
      "score": 97,
      "summary": "Output B labels every claim as a code fact, a runtime risk or a proposed move. Each finding has acceptance criteria. Its direct-manipulation advice preserves the grab offset and covers pointer capture, pointer IDs and touch-action. On interruption, it samples the current on-screen position and velocity before handing control back to the pointer. It measures velocity in CSS px/s with monotonic timestamps and gives an explicit projection formula. It also gives transform ownership its own layer and covers Reduced Motion in both CSS and JS. One weakness: putting snap targeting at P2 is debatable."
    },
    "emil": {
      "blind_label": "C",
      "criteria": {
        "accessibility": 10,
        "actionability": 9,
        "direct_manipulation": 15,
        "evidence_honesty": 14,
        "interruption": 15,
        "motion_necessity": 9,
        "performance": 9,
        "velocity_projection": 13
      },
      "score": 94,
      "summary": "Output C labels observed facts, expected effects and proposals well. Its ownership advice is strong, and its step-by-step grab, track, release, settle and cancel sequence is useful. It requires interruption from the displayed position, ignores stale velocity samples and adds hysteresis. Its Reduced Motion path is careful and notes that a CSS media query alone won't stop the JS animation. It gives slightly less detail than B on velocity units and on separating transform ownership."
    }
